[¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#g28-auto-home)
 G28 - Auto Home
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#description-4)
 Description

*   When you first start up your machine, it has no idea where the toolhead is positioned, so Marlin needs to use a procedure called “homing” to establish a known position. To do this, it moves each axis towards one end of its track until it triggers a switch, commonly called an “endstop”. Marlin knows where the endstops are, so once all the endstops have been triggered, the position is known.
*   The `G28` command is used to home one or more axes. The default behavior with no parameters is to home all axes.
*   The position is easy to lose when the steppers are turned off, so homing may be required or advised after the machine has been sitting idle for a period of time.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#notes-2)
 Notes

*   To prevent collisions, the machine will only home all axes for 3DP scenes.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#usage-4)
 Usage

    G28 [O] [X] [Y] [Z]

Copy

*   Parameters
    *   O: Flag to skip homing if the position is already trusted.
    *   X: Flag to home the X axis.
    *   Y: Flag to home the Y axis.
    *   Z: Flag to home the Z axis.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#examples-4)
 Examples

*   The most-used form of this command is to home all axes:
    
        G28    ; Home all axes
    
    Copy
    
        G28 X Z ; Home the X and Z axes
    
    Copy
    
        G28 O ; Home all "untrusted" axes
    
    Copy
    

[¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#g42-move-to-mesh-coordinate)
 G42 - Move to mesh coordinate
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#description-5)
 Description

*   The `G42` command moves the nozzle to the location corresponding to a specific coordinate in the bed leveling mesh. It operates similarly to the `G0` and `G1` commands except that the provided coordinates are a mesh row and column instead of an absolute or relative position on the bed.
*   The `G42` command will determine the bed position that corresponds to the provided mesh row and column and move the nozzle to that position.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#notes-3)
 Notes

*   Using `M420 V`, the information of the mesh can be obtained.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#usage-5)
 Usage

    G42 [F<rate>] [I<pos>] [J<pos>]

Copy

*   Parameters
    *   F: The maximum movement rate of the movement between the start and end point. The feedrate set here applies to subsequent moves that omit this parameter.
    *   I: The column of the mesh coordinate.
    *   J: The row of the mesh coordinate.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#examples-5)
 Examples

*   Move to various points on a 5x5 mesh.
    
        G42 I0 J0 ; front left corner
        G42 I2 J2 ; center
        G42 I2 J4 ; back center
        G42 I4 J4 ; back right
    
    Copy
    

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#g53-move-in-machine-coordinates)
 G53 - Move in Machine Coordinates

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#description-6)
 Description

*   `G53` applies native workspace to the current move. See `G54` - `G59.3` for workspace coordinate system. In CNC G-code `G53` is a modifier. It precedes a movement command (or other modifiers) on the same line. Marlin also accepts `G53` on a line by itself as the command to return to the native workspace.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#usage-6)
 Usage

    G53

Copy

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#examples-6)
 Examples

*   Rapid linear move to the machine origin in native space.
    
        G53 G0 X0 Y0 Z0
    
    Copy
    
*   Rapid linear move to absolute coordinate X20 in native space.
    
        G53 G1 X20
    
    Copy
    
*   Use native space
    
        G53
    
    Copy
    

[¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#g54-g593-workspace-coordinate-system)
 G54 - G59.3 - Workspace Coordinate System
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#description-7)
 Description

*   A workspace is an XYZ offset to the native machine space. All workspaces default to (0,0,0) at start, or with EEPROM support they may be restored from a previous session. See `G53` for native space.
    *   G92: used to set the current workspace’s offset.
    *   G54: use coordinate system 1.
    *   G55: use coordinate system 2.
    *   G56: use coordinate system 3.
    *   G57: use coordinate system 4.
    *   G58: use coordinate system 5.
    *   G59: use coordinate system 6.
    *   G59.1: use coordinate system 7.
    *   G59.2: use coordinate system 8.
    *   G59.3: use coordinate system 9.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#notes-4)
 Notes

*   Between SM2.0 and Luban, as well as between SM2.0's controller and screen, only `G53` and `G54` are recommended for use.

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#usage-7)
 Usage

    G54
    G55
    G56
    G57
    G58
    G59
    G59.1
    G59.2
    G59.3

Copy

### [¶](https://wiki.snapmaker.com/en/Snapmaker_Luban/manual/2_supported_gcode_references#examples-7)
 Examples

*   Use coordinate system 1 and rapid move to workspace offset origin:
    
        G54 ; Select workspace 1
        G0 X0 Y0 X0
    
    Copy
    

